# .gitignore — erpnext_construction_ph

# Python bytecode (stale .pyc can shadow source edits — see ARCHITECTURE.md §Reliability)
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd

# Frappe build artifacts
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
.eggs/

# intproject operational temp dirs (never commit gate evidence to repo)
# Temp dirs are scratch, but the `check.txt` inside them is NOT: AGENTS.md's
# close protocol requires `ops/tmp-<id>/check.txt` and GATES.md cites those
# paths as the authoritative evidence for every closed row. Excluding the
# whole directory meant a fresh clone had no evidence for any row while
# the gates still pointed at it. Exclude the contents, then re-include the
# evidence -- a negation cannot reach inside an excluded *directory*.
ops/tmp-*/*
!ops/tmp-*/check.txt
# A gate's CHECK may also NAME a script it runs (PH3-02: `node
# ops/tmp-ph3-02/geometry_check.mjs`). The line above re-includes evidence; this
# re-includes the runners, for the same reason: a gate that points at a file git
# ignores cannot be run from a fresh clone, so the row's own acceptance criterion
# is unrunnable by anyone but the session that wrote it.
#
# This was ONCE a per-file exception (`!ops/tmp-*/geometry_check.mjs` and one
# orphan probe), on the reasoning that a blanket exception would commit junk. The
# cost of that reasoning showed up in a single session: SEVEN gate scripts were
# authored and named by a CHECK line, and all seven stayed ignored, so every one of
# those gates was runnable only by the session that wrote it. A per-file exception
# makes "add a gate" and "make the gate runnable" two separate steps, and the second
# one gets forgotten -- which is the same defect as an ungated CHECK command, just
# quieter.
#
# So the shape is negated rather than the names. A row directory is an evidence
# directory: anything in it that a gate names must be runnable, and the real throwaway
# scratch is outside them.
!ops/tmp-*/*.py
!ops/tmp-*/*.mjs
# ...and the SHELL gates, which is where the gap was. The two lines above were written
# when the gates happened to be `.py` and `.mjs`, and `.sh` was not added -- so ELEVEN
# DONE rows (CORE-20, 21, 23, 24, 25, 26, 27, 29, 30, 31, 32) named a `.sh` gate that git
# ignored. Found by trying to commit a fix to CORE-30's gate and being told there was
# nothing to commit: the edit was real, the file was simply not tracked.
#
# The cost of a per-extension exception is the same one described above, one level up: it
# makes "write a gate" and "make the gate runnable" two steps, and the second gets
# forgotten. Every gate this project has written is a shell script, so this is the
# extension that matters, and it should have been here from the first one.
!ops/tmp-*/*.sh

# Same gap, one extension over: the B4 evidence for the truncated-posting finding is a .md
# (`ops/tmp-core38/ACC-SINV-03099-B4.md`), and BUILD_TRACKER.md and GATES.md both cite it.
# Every extension above was re-included for the same reason -- a cited artefact that is not
# tracked stops existing on a fresh clone while the ledger still names it. Force-adding would
# paper over this: the next edit to the ledger would show 'nothing to commit' and the evidence
# would silently stop being evidence.
!ops/tmp-*/*.md

# ...and `.js`, which was MISSING and cost more than the rest put together.
# `ops/tmp-ph3-05/browser.js` is the shared CDP helper that CORE-19's gate and both PH1
# browser gates `import`. Every one of those gates is a `DONE` row whose CHECK names a
# script that imports a file git ignores -- so on a fresh clone they cannot run at all,
# and the failure is a module-not-found rather than anything that names the cause.
#
# Found the hard way: a scoped-precondition fix landed in it, `git diff` reported the file
# UNCHANGED, and the reason was that for a gitignored path `git diff` reports nothing. The
# file had the fix in it the whole time and was not in `HEAD`. **An untracked file cannot
# be reviewed by reading the diff, because there is no diff.**
!ops/tmp-*/*.js
# A gate NOTE may cite a probe that produced its measurement (DOCS-04 section 5.7
# cites `ops/tmp-ph3-02/orphan_rows.py` as the read that root-caused the localhost
# 500). Citing a file git ignores leaves the next reader with a claim and no way to
# re-run it, which is the same defect as an ungated CHECK command. Scoped to the one
# named probe rather than a blanket `!ops/tmp-*/*.py`.
!ops/tmp-*/orphan_rows.py

# CORE-33's RAW EVIDENCE, named individually rather than by extension. Two attempts to
# record WHY those numbers are contaminated have now failed on the same gap: `.md` was not
# re-included, so an annotation file could not be committed. That is the third extension to
# fall through this list (`.py`, `.mjs`, `.sh`, `.js`, now `.md`), and three is enough to
# stop extending the list one line at a time.
#
# **The defect is the per-extension approach, not the missing entry.** Every one of those
# gaps was found the same way: something was fixed, measured green, and a reader later found
# the evidence was never in the repository. The extensions that matter here are exactly the
# ones a GATE produces -- scripts it runs, harnesses it drives, files it writes -- and they
# are already covered. Anything else belongs under `ops/tmp-*/` as scratch unless it is
# NAMED here, with the reason, so the list stays short enough to be read.
#
# These two are kept because they are the only trace of a contaminated measurement. Editing
# or deleting the figures would falsify the record; what is needed is that a reader can see
# them AND see that they are not the leak figure.
!ops/tmp-core33/before_core33.txt
!ops/tmp-core33/after_core33.txt
# Throwaway probes written directly into ops/ (not the row directories).
# This class keeps reappearing because `ops/tmp-*/*` only matches
# *inside* a directory, so a root-level `ops/tmp-anything.py` slips
# through and gets committed. Ending the pattern, not the instance.
ops/tmp-*.py
planning/tmp-*/

# Environment / secrets
.env
.env.*
*.local

# OS artifacts
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# IDE
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo

# Node (for Frappe frontend build)
node_modules/
.pnpm-store/

# BIM vendor bundles and WASM: GENERATED, 19 MB, and reproducible.
# Built by erpnext_construction_ph/.../tools/build_bim_vendor.mjs from the pinned
# `@thatopen/*` versions in package.json. Committed by accident once, which put
# 227k lines of generated JS and three ~1.3 MB WASM binaries into history for no
# benefit: a fresh clone runs the bundler and gets byte-identical output, and
# committing it means every dependency bump leaves an unreviewable diff.
# The same reasoning excludes the third-party .ifc fixture used by the PH3-02
# gate -- it is That Open's `school_str.ifc`, not this project's data, and it is
# re-fetchable from the URL the gate names.
erpnext_construction_ph/erpnext_construction_ph/public/js/bim/vendor/
erpnext_construction_ph/erpnext_construction_ph/*.ifc
erpnext_construction_ph/erpnext_construction_ph/*.frag

# Frappe bench artifacts (if bench lives inside repo root)
apps/
sites/
logs/
config/
env/
jar*.txt
sk*.txt
jar*.txt
ops/tmp-corebench/*.sql filter=diff merge=binary
*.sql

# A stray file-descriptor redirect (`2>&1` mis-typed, or `> 222`) lands a zero-byte file
# named for the fd in the repo root. Twice now: 222 and 229. Nothing legitimate here is a
# bare number, so refuse them at the door rather than letting them show up as untracked.
/[0-9]*

# --- Internal engineering record — NOT published (added 2026-10-05) -------------
# These were removed from git HISTORY, not merely untracked, because they carry
# candid internal material: named owners on blocked rows, unresolved class-A findings,
# and literal local dev credentials. They stay on disk and stay in use; they are just
# no longer part of what is published. See C:\construction-erp-backup-20261005-1149.
/ops/
/GATES.md
/BUILD_TRACKER.md
/AGENTS.md
/BUILD-DESCRIPTION-*.md
